{"event":"trap","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-1/branch-6","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-1/branch-6/trap-0","ordinal":0,"type":"job","arg":{"spec":"briefing-relate","input":{"paper":{"id":"2611.17930","title":"Token2Tile: Token-Wise Sparse Attention with Tile Execution through a Low-Rank Space for Video DiTs","abstract":"Attention is essential for modeling spatio-temporal dependencies in video diffusion transformers, but its quadratic cost makes it a major inference bottleneck. Its high sparsity offers an opportunity to accelerate generation by focusing computation on important interactions. However, accuracy and execution favor different granularities: each query attends to its own key tokens, whereas GPU kernels work on tiles. We present Token2Tile, a training-free sparse attention operator that combines per-query scoring and a per-query residual with tile execution. An online low-rank space, computed directly from queries and keys to preserve attention logits, makes clustering, scoring, and residual approximation efficient. Token2Tile unites query-specific selections to allocate exact work across query clusters, then packs the selected keys into GPU tiles. On MiniMax-H3 and Wan2.1-14B, Token2Tile delivers 1.48-1.54x end-to-end speedup over dense FlashAttention-3 with VBench scores within 0.5 points of dense. At comparable speeds on H3, it improves PSNR over SOL and SubBlock by 5.5-6.8 dB; on Wan, it exceeds SOL by 4.6-4.8 dB while running faster.","authors":"Xuecheng Wu , Aichen Feng , Jinyan Chen , Qingjie Zhao , Julien Lai","topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"]},"priorWork":[{"id":"2609.26333","title":"Disaggregated Quantization: Specializing LLM Prefill and Decode","date":"2026-09-25"},{"id":"2609.24197","title":"H-Spec: Parallel Speculative Decoding Without a Drafter-Side KV Cache","date":"2026-09-25"},{"id":"2609.27981","title":"Risk-Controlled KV-Cache Eviction: From Memory Budgets to Risk Targets","date":